WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.280
Fairies are everywhere and seem to get into&nbsp;
pretty much everything. They can be tricksters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.560
or guardians, jealous or benevolent. Some of the&nbsp;
oldest fairies of the land are the fae folk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.440
Irish, Welsh, and Scottish Celtic traditions.
These fairies of Celtic traditions trace their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:21.840
origin back to an ancient race of otherworldly&nbsp;
royalty - the Tuatha Dé Danann. As fairy nobility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.000
the Tuatha De Danann were alternatively&nbsp;
seen as gods, monsters, demons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.040
and even the real ancient inhabitants of Ireland.
But where do the Tuatha Dé Danann come from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.920
and how did they become the&nbsp;
ancestors of Ireland’s fairies?

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.960
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka and this is Monstrum

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.640
In Gaelic the Tuatha Dé Danann roughly translates&nbsp;
to People or Nation of the Goddess Danu.&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.360
According to Irish folklorist W. B.&nbsp;
Yeats’ influential folklore collection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.320
Tuatha De Danann were “trooping fairies…'' “fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.000
angels who were not good enough to&nbsp;
be saved, nor bad enough to be lost.”

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.600
They played games, held funerals,&nbsp;
and loved music and dance.&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.280
Although they are known for their&nbsp;
duplicitous nature towards humans&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.680
willing to help or hurt in seemingly equal&nbsp;
measure. They’re essentially royal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.840
warrior, fairy gods that shapeshift&nbsp;
into whatever form they wish.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.480
The Tuatha Dé Danann may or may not be deities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:18.400
ancient Celtic spiritual practices,&nbsp;
but they are certainly god-like.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.400
Not only because of their preternatural&nbsp;
beauty - as shapeshifters they can appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.320
as human-like creatures with&nbsp;
yellowed and wizened faces&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.440
but their exceptional skills in everything from art&nbsp;
to smithery, magic to poetry set them apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.360
They are also called “the Ever-Living&nbsp;
Ones,” which suggests their immortality.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.000
Seanchaí undoubtedly&nbsp;
related tales of the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.280
beings in oral tradition before&nbsp;
they appeared in written texts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.280
likely in regard to existing beliefs&nbsp;
in Druid power and supernatural magic.&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.920
But when Christianity arrived in Ireland,&nbsp;
this ancient race of god-like beings had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.000
conquered, made less powerful than the&nbsp;
Christian God by turning them into more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.320
folkloric figures—although the Church seemed&nbsp;
confused about exactly how to do that.&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.080
For instance, the first written mention of&nbsp;
the creatures appearing in The Book of Armagh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.720
in the 9th-century, refers to the Tuatha&nbsp;
De Danann as “the gods of the earth.”

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.200
They are said to have descended from heaven&nbsp;
on great clouds, cast out for their knowledge.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.360
Yet in the 10th century, Christian scribes&nbsp;
like Eochaid ua Flainn began to question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.760
what he called “phantoms,” unsure if&nbsp;
they were of demonic or human origins.&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.800
Another century later we find the first written&nbsp;
recording of the mythological origins of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.160
Tuatha Dé Dannan in the anonymous 11th-century&nbsp;
CE text Book of Invasions

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.040
The text collects the so-called history of Ireland&nbsp;
in what we would now refer to as ‘euhemerism,’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.160
which is basically interpreting&nbsp;
myths as real historical events.&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:44.080
I’m going to do my best here, but the mythology&nbsp;
is really complicated. So bear with me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.320
This is only a snippet of the whole story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.200
so maybe we’ll explore more of it in our sister&nbsp;
show Fate &amp; Fabled. Get ready to take notes.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.160
According to the myth, the Tuatha Dé Danann&nbsp;
arrived on a distant western mountain in Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.200
In some stories they disembark from dark clouds&nbsp;
so large, they caused a three-day eclipse.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.600
In other versions, they approach the spot&nbsp;
by sea before burning their ships on land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.480
in a fire so great its smoke caused the eclipse.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.200
Either way, when the Tuatha Dé Dannan arrived&nbsp;
in Ireland, they found it already inhabited.&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.240
Occupying the land were the Fir Bolg, humans&nbsp;
once enslaved in Greece, and the Fomorians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.800
a supernatural race considered destructive&nbsp;
deities or demonic bandits—take your pick.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.800
Of course, a great battle ensued. The ungodly&nbsp;
powerful Tuatha Dé Danann were victorious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.120
and under their rule came a time of peace and&nbsp;
prosperity. In part because they intermarried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.320
with the other preternatural races on the island.
They ruled for 169 years - until the mortal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.760
Milesians arrived. Even with their magic, the&nbsp;
Tuatha Dé Danann could not defeat these humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.600
After the conflict, the surface&nbsp;
of Ireland went to the mortals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.960
the underground or Otherworld&nbsp;
to the “Ever Living Ones.”
&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.920
The surviving Tuatha Dé Danann fled to their new&nbsp;
home through ancient hills, barrows, and burial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.520
mounds called sidhe. These spaces are said to&nbsp;
be doorways for dead souls and the fae alike.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.840
As they became more secluded and more&nbsp;
secretive, they increasingly became perceived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.400
as less mischievous and more malevolent.
Over time, the royal race of Tuatha Dé Danann&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.480
blurred with their more broadly categorized&nbsp;
offspring, the “people of the sidhe,” - the Aos sí.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.680
These more generalized fairy folk are commonly&nbsp;
called just the Sidhe, a shorthand referring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.377
to the earthen mounds the Tuatha Dé Danann and&nbsp;
their descendants use to transport between worlds.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.160
As Christianity grew, it’s thought that the belief&nbsp;
in the Aos sí moved further and further away 

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.760
from their&nbsp;ancestors the Tuatha Dé Dannan. Eventually, some&nbsp;even became taxonomized into different subspecies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.960
or populations of fairies. Like the leprechaun,&nbsp;
the pooka, and the Dullahan to name a few.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.920
Omnipresent in Celtic folklore, the fae folk&nbsp;
appear in many forms and under many names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:51.440
such as the “Other Crowd,” “the&nbsp;
Good People,” or simply “Them.”

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:57.520
But they did not disappear. Many believe fae folk&nbsp;
are still there, a “secret civilization” hiding in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.400
plain sight in Ireland even today. Commonly,&nbsp;
the Otherworld is perceived as an idyllic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.280
mirror version of our own. But that doesn’t&nbsp;
mean the Celtic fae folk can’t be monstrous.&nbsp;

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.920
Tradition claims that if the Tuatha Dé Danann were&nbsp;
angered they would withhold important resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.120
like milk and corn, until humans appeased them&nbsp;
with sacrifices of food, humans, and animals.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.120
This is one reason offerings of food and&nbsp;
drink are still made to fairies today.&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.520
Twilight is the time of day&nbsp;
when one is most likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.240
encounter the Tuatha Dé Danann or the Aos sí.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.800
While not everyone encounters them, humans&nbsp;
in transitional states are the most at risk:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.120
pregnant women and their babies, nursing&nbsp;
mothers, those starting puberty, and the sick.&nbsp;

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:39.440
The Aos sí also happen to be rather fond&nbsp;
of what we would think of as kidnapping.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:43.920
Horses, wildlife, livestock, humans - the&nbsp;
Good People are known to “carry off”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.240
lives from the human world. Sometimes they&nbsp;
even switch in a replacement of their own.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.600
When humans are swapped, they are said&nbsp;
to be replaced by a changeling&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.440
weak,&nbsp;sickly fairies that look identical&nbsp;
to the humans they replaced

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.240
 (thanks&nbsp;to those ancestral shapeshifting powers).
While cruel and even violent, they also have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.120
a sense of justice and can be incredibly&nbsp;
helpful to humans. To quote Yeats again:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.080
“They are good to the good, and evil to the evil.”
Like in the “The Story-Teller at Fault.”

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.480
A human king’s storyteller&nbsp;
encounters what appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.840
be a lanky old beggarman with&nbsp;
a wooden leg in his garden.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.160
The strange ‘old man’ offers to play a&nbsp;
dice game with the storyteller who agrees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.400
to a wager of 100 pieces of gold [they&nbsp;
play on a smooth stone set before them].&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.920
The storyteller loses and loses again and&nbsp;
again his wagers becoming more dramatic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:33.360
From his horses to his hounds, and eventually&nbsp;
even his wife, he loses everything. 

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.360
Finally, the storyteller wages the only thing of value&nbsp;
he has left - his own life. He of course loses.&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.400
The old man pulls out a long cord and a wand&nbsp;
from his pocket, revealing he is one of the Other&nbsp;People

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.040
and asks the storyteller what kind&nbsp;
of animal he would like to be turned into.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.040
The story-teller chooses a hare, and&nbsp;
without delay, the supernatural being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.920
throws the cord around him and strikes him&nbsp;
with a wand, transforming human into hare.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.000
Chased by his own hounds the Aos sí takes&nbsp;
pity upon him and turns him back into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.360
a human. He even allows the&nbsp;
story-teller to accompany him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.520
unseen, as he tricks more humans&nbsp;
and performs great magical feats.
 

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:13.520
On his journey, the Aos sí offends&nbsp;
the human king, who orders him hanged.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:17.280
Again and again, the king’s men hang&nbsp;
the creature, seemingly killing him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.520
but every time, he reappears unharmed—with someone&nbsp;
the king values hanging from the gallows instead.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:28.640
The King finally shows the supernatural being&nbsp;
respect, and appeased by his rational decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.200
the Aos sí restores life to the men he had&nbsp;
killed. And reveals his identity to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:39.280
story-teller—he is not just any old Aos sí but&nbsp;
Angus of the Bruff, one of the Tuatha Dé Danann.&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:43.680
This story is typical behavior of the Tuatha&nbsp;
Dé Danann and their offspring as a whole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.720
Capable of great magic, including transfiguration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:52.400
Angus is tricky and can be cruel. But he can&nbsp;
also be fair. He reverses all the bad he does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.640
but only when humans act respectfully.
These stories help explain misfortune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:02.320
illness, economic loss, even death. Performing&nbsp;
small rituals to appease the Good People,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:06.960
ward them off, or ask for their help, gives&nbsp;
humans a sense of control over that which cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.040
be controlled. It’s comforting in a way.
Perhaps that’s part of the reason why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.280
stories of the Aos sí continued to&nbsp;
thrive even as their ranks changed.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:19.360
Renaissance artists, for one, were fond of&nbsp;
depicting them. And in the late nineteenth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.080
early 20th centuries, the “Irish Renaissance”&nbsp;
or “Celtic Revival'' meant that many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.400
older tales were finally written down.
Irish folklore collectors like Yeats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.760
Thomas Crofton Croker, Lady Gregory, and Jeremiah&nbsp;
Curtin preserved fairy stories and other tales of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:39.040
the supernatural in print, making it possible&nbsp;
for greater circulation of the narratives.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:42.095
Just look at all the fairy stories out there&nbsp;even now.

00:08:42.095 --> 00:08:45.120
Today, while fewer and fewer Irish people will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.800
claim they believe in fairies, many still&nbsp;
know of the superstitions and stories of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:54.000
the Aos sí and the ancient Tuatha Dé Danann.
Oral storytelling through narrative and song was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.840
and still is, crucial to Irish culture. Go to any&nbsp;
good pub in Ireland and that will become apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.720
very quickly. While the magical, supernatural&nbsp;
fae folk seem best suited for the pages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.160
your favorite fantasy novel, they are still&nbsp;
undoubtedly a very real part of Irish history.

